# AGENTS.md

Instructions for AI agents working in this repository.

## Purpose

An academic paper (or report) written in Markdown. The deliverable is
Word (`.docx`) generated by Pandoc. Shared tools come from trazo
(`scripts/trazo`, version pinned in `trazo.toml`).

## Rules

- Keep documentation short, practical, and in English.
- Prefer small, targeted edits over broad rewrites.
- Do not edit generated artifacts by hand. Change the source and
  regenerate:
  - figures: `figures/scripts/`
  - tables: `tables/scripts/` (numbers, written to `tables/output/data/`)
    and `tables/specs/<label>.json` (presentation; edit with
    `scripts/trazo table patch`, check with
    `scripts/trazo table check --all --outputs`)
  - numbers in prose: `{{v:key}}` placeholders, computed by
    `scripts/build_values.py` into `data/values.json`
  - diagrams: `figures/diagrams/<name>.json`, then
    `scripts/trazo diagram check NAME` and `export NAME`; never edit
    `figures/external/` by hand
- Do not invent citations. A citation key used in the manuscript must
  exist in `bibliography/references.bib`.
- Do not hard-code figure or table numbers. Use cross-reference labels:
  `[@fig:name]`, `[@tbl:name]`, `{#fig:name}`, `{#tbl:name}`. A label in
  the other document (manuscript or supplementary) resolves there, so
  "Figure S1" is `[@fig:name]` too.
- Preserve Pandoc citation syntax: `[@key]`, `@key`,
  `[@key1; @key2]`.
- Do not edit trazo's code or skills here; they come from
  trazo. Project-only skills go in `agent-workflows/skills/`.

## Build

Builds do not use Make; never run `make` (a Makefile an older template
left is stale, and on a clean Mac `make` is an installer stub). When the
trazo tools are in this session (the desktop apps' plugin), use them;
otherwise, and for what they do not cover, use `scripts/trazo` from the
project root.
- Word files: `build` (`scripts/trazo build`; one document:
  `scripts/trazo build --doc manuscript`).
- Submission files (articles): `build` with `submission`, then `check`. The
  call succeeds even when the report has errors: read its `errors`.
- Preview and diagram editor: `preview_start` (a free port when 8765 is
  busy). Open the URL it returns (also in `.build/preview.json`); the editor
  is at `/diagrams` on it. Each call first regenerates the values, tables
  and figures whose data or scripts changed, so call it again after such a
  change.
- Required tools: `doctor`.
- The rest, with `scripts/trazo`: `verify` (clean, regenerate, build and
  check everything); citations, `build --prepare=inputs` then `citations`;
  `counts` and `citations --summary` after the same prepare; `agents`
  regenerates the Codex and Claude skill layouts.

In a terminal, the same with `scripts/trazo`:

```bash
scripts/trazo verify                  # clean, regenerate, build and check
scripts/trazo build                   # every Word file
scripts/trazo build --submission      # and the separate submission files
scripts/trazo submission check        # what an article still needs
scripts/trazo build --prepare=inputs && scripts/trazo preview serve
                                         # live preview at 127.0.0.1:8765
scripts/trazo doctor                  # check uv, Python, Pandoc and Chrome
```

Required tools are uv and Pandoc (`scripts/trazo setup` downloads
Pandoc) and, for diagrams, exports and `scripts/trazo verify`, Google
Chrome, Edge or Chromium (on a Mac with none, `scripts/trazo setup`
downloads a headless Chrome). uv manages Python 3.12 and the project's
Python dependencies.

## Editing Workflow

1. Inspect the relevant files before editing.
2. Make the smallest useful change.
3. Regenerate figures, tables or values if their sources changed.
4. Run `scripts/trazo verify` before claiming the paper-generation
   workflow works.
5. Report exact commands run and whether they passed.
6. Commit the finished change when git is available and this folder is a
   repository; otherwise say nothing about git.

After a template update (the plugin's `paper_update`, or `scripts/trazo
update`), a `<file>.new` holds the template's version of a file trazo
could not update safely, usually one the project changed. Merge it into the
file, keeping the project's changes, then delete it. Then run
`scripts/trazo verify`.

Review comments made in the live preview are answered with the
`comments` skill: list all scopes with `scripts/trazo review comments`,
edit the sources, then `scripts/trazo review resolve SCOPE:ID "reply"`. The
author may edit the same files from the preview page; re-read a file
before editing it.

Use uv for Python environment and dependency management. Update
`pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock` together when dependencies change. Run
Python through `scripts/trazo` or `.venv/bin/python` (in a terminal, also
`uv run python ...`), never a system Python.

## Manuscript Content

- Manuscript files live in `manuscript/main/`.
- Supplementary files live in `manuscript/supplementary/`.
- Metadata lives in `manuscript/metadata.yaml`.
- For article/report kind, authors, affiliations and keywords, see README's
  “Document metadata” section.
- References live in `bibliography/references.bib`.
- For articles, use `journal-submission` to configure journal placement and limits;
  keep stable `decl-` ids when editing declaration headings. Build the
  submission files with `build` and `submission`, then `check`.
- Before submitting an article, run `check` (in a terminal,
  `scripts/trazo submission check --strict`) and fix every error it lists.
- Never invent ethics approvals, funding, competing interests or contributions;
  ask the authors and keep unresolved facts as `[PLACEHOLDER: …]`.

When writing Methods or Results for a scientific paper, prefer precise,
evidence-matched prose and avoid unsupported claims.
